{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791142266,"nonce":"geQxzjjMPAYpxR5SrBrrfdYjLRQ=","message":"Done. GenericFont::System now resolves to the platform's own interface\nface, which is what system-ui means and what a browser resolves it to,\nbefore falling back to the generic sans.\n\nOrder tried: macOS \".SF NS\", \".SF NS Text\", \"SF Pro Text\", \"SF Pro\",\n\"Helvetica Neue\"; Windows \"Segoe UI Variable Text\", \"Segoe UI\"; Linux\n\"Cantarell\", \"Ubuntu\", \"Noto Sans\", \"DejaVu Sans\", since there is no one\nanswer there. Anything not installed falls through to Family::SansSerif,\nso a machine without these is no worse off than before.\n\nConfirmed on macOS: System resolves to .SF NS, it carries a wght axis,\nand the axis follows the requested weight — 300, 400 and 700 each come\nback with their own value. That is exactly why b02669c had to land\nfirst: without it every weight would have drawn at the default\ninstance.\n\nBoth prerequisites were real and both are in:\n- b02669c, variable weights, or SF Pro would render regular everywhere.\n- 2506cd7, CSS line-height, or SF Pro's ~1.19-1.21em natural line height\n  would have made every line roughly a fifth taller.\n\nVerified on screen, not only in tests. The capture scene renders in SF\nPro with baselines still aligned across sizes and families, button text\nstill centred, and bold rendering genuinely bold through the axis rather\nthan falling back to regular. cn_demo renders in SF Pro throughout and\nlooks native.\n\nThe registry-level tests in blinc_text/tests/variable_weight.rs, which\nskipped while the generics all resolved to static families, are now live\nand passing.\n\nThis affects every text with no explicit family, since\nFontFamily::default() is GenericFont::System.","files":null}]}